68 #edb/ref 17592186485454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485535 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $AB+CB$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{7} & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59d9ac6b-9ad1-5629-b5da-d053470d3899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485535 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+CB=(A+C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+CB & =(A+C)B \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =5B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & 5 & 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 15 & 25 & 5 \\\\ -10\\sqrt{7} & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-319197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $4$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fdc95ea-e1eb-5331-974c-7dbe555559e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485538 #edb/ref 1074 "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485543 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72e0b567-7c07-513e-b4b8-2088d6f698dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485543 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}C \\\\  & =3C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 6 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 6 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485559 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n\nII. $(CA)B=C(AB)$\n\nIII. $BC=CB$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96d290b8-46cc-5702-9916-bb75bce9f305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485559 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is asociative, so $(CA)B=C(AB)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I  and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1069 "q-320017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4c5958e-629a-595b-ba26-53d736ebe0b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485578 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$ gives a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which would require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$ gives a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which requires finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
